package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part story: The Last Page of the Yearbook. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch49 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_last_page_of_the_yearbook",
            title = "The Last Page of the Yearbook",
            tagline = "Students completing a school yearbook uncover the story behind a page that was left blank for a reason.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "A Blank Square", listOf(
                    "The yearbook committee was preparing its final edition when Ada noticed a blank square near the back. Every other page had photographs, captions, or messages. The blank space had a thin border and a small heading: TO BE COMPLETED BY THE NEXT CLASS. Nobody knew why it had been left there.",
                    "The teacher adviser, Mr Lawal, checked the old production files. The page had appeared in every yearbook for a decade, but its purpose was never explained. Ada and her friend Nuru searched previous editions and found small differences: one year had a paper star taped over the square, another had a pressed leaf, and another had a tiny drawing of a door."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Committee Notes", listOf(
                    "The archive cupboard held minutes from past yearbook committees. The notes said the blank page was reserved for a message chosen by students who would graduate the following year. The idea began when a class asked to leave advice for younger students, but the committee wanted each new class to decide what mattered rather than inherit a fixed slogan.",
                    "Ada liked the tradition, but wondered why nobody had explained it. Nuru found a note from the first committee: 'A space is not empty if someone is invited to use it.' The current committee decided to ask former students what they remembered, while keeping the page blank until the present class had made its own choice."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "A Message in Pencil", listOf(
                    "A former student named Kemi replied to the alumni office. She remembered the blank square and said her class had chosen to leave it open because they could not agree on one message. Instead, they wrote a question for the next class: 'What do you wish someone had told you?' The question was printed on a separate insert, but the insert had gone missing.",
                    "The committee searched the print archive and found a proof with the question in the margin. It had been removed from the final edition because the printer was short on space. The blank square remained as a reminder of the original plan. Ada suggested that the new edition could restore the question, but the class should still decide whether to use it."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "Different Answers", listOf(
                    "The committee collected anonymous responses from students across year groups. Some wanted advice about organising schoolwork; others wrote about asking questions, joining clubs, and being patient with new experiences. A few said they wished adults had explained where to find support when school felt difficult. The teacher adviser reviewed the responses to remove identifying details.",
                    "Ada noticed that the most useful messages were specific and kind. One student wrote that it helped to ask a teacher to explain an assignment in another way. Another said that joining a club made the school feel smaller. The committee grouped the responses by theme, making sure the final page would not turn anyone's private experience into public material."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Old Photograph", listOf(
                    "A photograph from the first yearbook committee showed students gathered around a table, cutting paper letters for the blank square. The caption named the committee but did not explain the tradition. Mr Lawal contacted the retired teacher who had supervised that first edition. She remembered that the students wanted to leave room for future voices.",
                    "The retired teacher explained that the blank page had been a deliberate design choice. The school had changed many times, and each graduating class had different experiences. She hoped the tradition would remind students that a yearbook was not only a record of what happened; it was also a message to people who would arrive later. Ada asked whether the current class could preserve that purpose."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "The Debate", listOf(
                    "Some committee members wanted to fill the square with a polished quote. Others preferred a collage of short advice. Nuru suggested keeping the original question and adding a few anonymous responses around it. The group discussed readability, consent, and whether the page should include a date so future classes would understand when the advice was written.",
                    "They agreed on a simple layout: the question at the top, five short responses selected by the committee, and a note inviting the next class to make its own choice. Every response was checked with its contributor, and no names would appear. The page would remain recognisably open rather than becoming a permanent list of rules."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Proof", listOf(
                    "The printer sent a digital proof. Ada noticed that one response had been changed during formatting and no longer meant what its author intended. The committee corrected it and asked the printer to confirm the final text before production. Mr Lawal praised the students for checking meaning, not just spelling.",
                    "They also tested the page in black and white to ensure the design remained clear if copies were printed without colour. The blank border was kept, and the date was added beneath the question. The final proof included a small line: 'The next class may change this page.' It was a simple way to keep the tradition alive without forcing anyone to follow the same format."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The Launch", listOf(
                    "At the yearbook launch, Ada explained the page's history. Kemi attended as an invited former student and recognised the restored question. She said the new version captured what the first committee had hoped for: one class speaking to another without pretending to know exactly what the future would bring.",
                    "Students read the anonymous advice and added their own thoughts to a board in the hall. The committee made clear that the board was optional and that no one needed to share a personal story. The launch became a celebration of the year, but the blank square drew people into a conversation about what they wanted the next class to remember."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Next Committee", listOf(
                    "Before the school year ended, Ada and Nuru met the incoming yearbook team. They showed them the print files, the consent checklist, and the old committee notes. They explained that the blank page belonged to the next class, not to the current editors. The new team could keep it, change it, or create another way to invite future students to contribute.",
                    "The incoming committee asked to preserve the question but collect new answers next year. Ada wrote down the process and left the original notes in the archive. She was glad the tradition did not depend on one person remembering every detail. It had become something the school could explain, review, and choose again."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "Room for Tomorrow", listOf(
                    "The final yearbook included photographs of performances, projects, teams, and everyday school moments. At the back, the question waited above five thoughtful responses. The page looked complete, but its border left space around the words. Ada realised that the empty area was part of the message: a school year could be remembered without deciding what every future student should think.",
                    "On the last day, the committee handed the new team a folder marked YEARBOOK TRADITION—READ BEFORE EDITING. The blank square had begun as a disagreement, become a question, and turned into an invitation. The yearbook preserved the class that had just finished, but it also welcomed the class that had not yet arrived. Its last page was not an ending. It was room for tomorrow."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
